{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > collab/jolt/2026-11-05-workload-w-clean-implementation.findings.md <<'EOF'\n# Workload W clean implementation — 2026-11-05\n\nReplaced \"'`src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj` entirely, replaced its independent\nBabashka supervisor, and updated the existing `workload-w:jolt` task to invoke\nthe supervisor directly. Run remotely with `bb workload-w:jolt`. There are no\nquick modes, configurable sample counts, or reduced-duration success paths.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- One jolt socket owner uses one adapter instance for the listener and B'\"'s\n  adapter client, so the adapter pump rotates over all those descriptors.\n  The listener binds \"'`127.0.0.1:0`, advertises `[cbor/profile]`, and sets the\n  4,194,304-byte limit. Both paths echo binary payloads; text is a protocol\n  error. A'\"'s upgrade is generated and checked using the adapter's protocol\n  helpers over a separate raw native TCP socket.\n- A sends one masked 4,194,304-byte binary message. Small writes are scheduled\n  every 10 ms, with no accumulated byte credit or catch-up burst. The final\n  byte cannot be sent before 120 seconds after the first positive write.\n  Partial writes resume at the unsent offset; every native send receives a\n  pointer allocated with \"'`ffi/alloc`. Five hundred consecutive zero-progress\n  attempts report `TRANSFER STALLED` and throw, aborting the transfer leg.\n  A'\"'s server delivery count, byte count, delivery time, and raw echo count\n  are recorded separately.\n- B's dedicated \"'`Thread` maintains the absolute grid\n  `A-first-positive-write + (slot-id + 1) * 100 ms`. It publishes offers through\n  a stream. The owner sends through `wsj/connect!`'\"'s seam, records \"'`sent-at`,\n  and returns completion acknowledgements through a second stream. At most\n  eight reservations include both queued offers and sent requests. The B\n  thread never calls the adapter or mutates owner accounting.\n- Every slot, including missed slots, advances its original ID. Late slots\n  and slots refused by the outstanding cap are recorded as missed; neither\n  schedules nor cohort membership are reset. IDs 0–599 are the prospective\n  cohort. Missing cohort replies fail the cohort gate; fewer than 500\n  completed cohort samples produce `:invalid` and a nonzero exit.\n- Every received reply records all three timestamps and validates the full\n  1 KiB payload. Duplicate, unexpected, or malformed replies fail accounting.\n  Both p99s use the nearest-rank definition. A violation means either latency\n  exceeds 250 ms. The confidence gate computes the exact one-sided 95%\n  Clopper–Pearson upper bound, using the specified closed form for zero\n  violations and binomial-CDF inversion otherwise. Thus two violations at\n  n=600 cannot pass merely because the empirical p99 still passes.\n- After A'\"'s send and server delivery, B stops offering and gets up to five\n  seconds to drain already offered requests and receive A's echo. Every sent\n  B request, including those outside the cohort, must complete.\n- The REPL boots through \"'`yin.repl.main/boot` with `yin.repl.host/websocket`.\n  The finite loop is submitted exactly once after A'\"'s first positive write.\n  Every owner round calls \"'`driver/repl-step` and `driver/take-outbox`; both\n  the completed driver-step count and the actual submission result are\n  checked. See the REPL limitation below.\n- The independent Babashka supervisor starts the 300-second clock before\n  launching jolt and forcibly kills that process at the cap. The shell\n  `exec`s jolt, so there is no intervening shell process to kill instead.\n  A timeout, an exception, an invalid run, or a missing successful final\n  report returns nonzero with a `FAILURE workload-w` report.\n- Replies are flushed as `W-SAMPLE` records. Owner snapshots are flushed as\n  `W-STATE`; independently, every 10 seconds the supervisor flushes\n  `W-PROGRESS` with both running cohort p99s and the sample count. It retains\n  owner rounds, B counters, A bytes/percentage/window, REPL steps, and cursor\n  observations even if the owner hangs; snapshot age distinguishes stale\n  observations from live counters.\n\n## Local gates\n\nOnly cljstyle was executed locally, as requested. The workload and all runtime\nand integration gates belong to the remote orchestrator.\n\nCommand (installed cljstyle 0.17.642):\n\n```sh\ncljstyle check src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj src/dev/jolt_workload_w_run.clj bb.edn\n```\n\n| Gate | Local result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Harness cljstyle | PASS, exit 0 |\n| Supervisor cljstyle | PASS, exit 0 |\n| bb.edn cljstyle | PASS, exit 0 |\n| Jolt namespace loading / native API execution | NOT RUN — remote |\n| A delivery, raw echo, pacing, stall guard | NOT RUN — remote |\n| B prospective cohort / validity floor / both p99s / confidence | NOT RUN — remote |\n| B independent clock, cap, missed offers, lost replies | NOT RUN — remote |\n| REPL submission and per-round driver stepping | NOT RUN — remote |\n| Watchdog termination and independent sampler under a hung owner | NOT RUN — remote |\n\nStatic source inspection: no frozen adapter or REPL files were edited, no JVM\nmetadata type hints were introduced, and the worker'\"'s application requires\nresolve under \"'`src/jolt` or `src/cljc`. `jolt.ffi`, `jolt.io-poller`, and\n`jolt.socket.native` are the jolt runtime'\"'s own namespaces. The supervisor is a\nBabashka program and is never loaded by jolt. No runtime compatibility or\nbenchmark result is claimed by the formatting gate.\n\n## Interpretation and deviations\n\n1. **REPL evaluation is synchronous in the frozen implementation.**\n   \"'`driver/repl-step` evaluates a submitted local form through\n   `yin.repl/eval-input`, which runs the VM synchronously. It exposes no\n   resumable per-round evaluation of an ordinary submitted loop. The harness\n   submits the finite loop once during A'\"'s transfer, verifies its result,\n   and counts actual successful driver-step calls every round. These counts\n   do **not** demonstrate VM instruction progress or continued loop evaluation\n   in later rounds. The runtime report labels the measurement \"'`:driver-steps`\n   and includes this limitation. If the registration requires an actively\n   evaluating VM loop in every owner round, this implementation cannot certify\n   that condition with the frozen driver API; such a reading requires a\n   separate architectural decision rather than silently resubmitting input or\n   fabricating VM progress.\n2. **The requested cursor locations are not exposed in this checkout.**\n   Telemetry reads `[:repl :indexer :materialization :cursor]` on the driver\n   state and `[:index-publication :cursor]` on `yin.repl/repl-state`'\"'s shell\n   summary. The frozen indexer/summary do not currently contain those fields,\n   so their honest observation is \"'`nil`. No other cursor is substituted.\n3. **A'\"'s 120 seconds is the nominal pacing duration and a hard minimum.**\n   Late owner visits or short/would-block writes extend the transfer rather\n   than creating a catch-up burst. The observed start, final send, and delivery\n   times remain in the report; the whole-process cap still applies.\n4. **Statistical formula clarification.** The supplied\n   \"'`1 - 0.05''^(1/n)` is the exact bound for zero violations. Its generalization\n   for observed violations is used to implement the supplied “at n=600: at\n   most 1 violation” rule, rather than incorrectly applying the zero-event\n   formula regardless of observed violations.\nEOF\n/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/cljstyle/0.17.642/cljstyle check src/dev/jolt_workload_w.clj src/dev/jolt_workload_w_run.clj bb.edn'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
